## COMMON RULES (apply to every phase)

Project: KroGram, an independent, unofficial Telegram client for iOS (Swift/SwiftUI, iOS 17+, Swift 5.9 mode) on TDLib via `TDLibFramework`. XcodeGen (`KroGram/project.yml`), Codemagic (`codemagic.yaml`). The owner works from an iPhone only and verifies through GitHub + Codemagic logs.

Product goal: a modified Telegram, not a different app. Behavior and navigation follow Telegram on iOS; the visual identity (neon, glass/blur, themes, backgrounds, Arabic RTL + English) and extras (Ghost, Commander, local manual save) are KroGram's. KroGram-only screens live inside Settings or folders, not as extra main tabs. All colors come from the theme engine (`KroTheme` / `ThemeStore`); no hard-coded neon in screens.

Round discipline
1. Read the repo as it is now and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`. Post a short plan and expected build risks before code.
2. Do exactly ONE phase per round, in the listed order of steps, one commit per step with a short note (design/root cause, what changed, how tested).
3. Never claim something works, builds or passes tests without real evidence (CI log, test output). State what you could not verify.
4. No fake code and no screens that look working but are not wired. Unsupported = hidden or a clear "not supported in this version" card.
5. Regression gate at the start of every phase: confirm signed-in state survives network loss, chat list and history stay paginated, avatars load on demand, memory caps still hold. If untested, say so.

Build safety (no compiler on your side; code must compile the first time)
- Split complex SwiftUI expressions into small properties/functions.
- Never `.listRowBackground(.ultraThinMaterial)`; use `Rectangle().fill(.ultraThinMaterial)`.
- Grep the repo for every type/method before using it. Verify every TDLib request/field name against the official TDLib docs. No new packages without stating reason, license, size and CI impact, and getting approval.
- Every new string in BOTH `ar.lproj` and `en.lproj`, no duplicates/missing keys. Unit tests for all new logic; tests must compile.

Security and privacy
- No `api_id`, `api_hash`, login codes, passwords or session data in source, logs or GitHub; secrets only in Keychain. Never log message content, codes or session data.
- No user data to any server other than Telegram. External services (translation, AI) are optional, off by default, disclosed.
- Do not claim regular chats are end-to-end encrypted; only Secret Chats are.
- Keep the visible "independent, unofficial client" notice. Do not copy code or assets from Telegram-iOS (GPL) or Telegram's icons/stickers/sounds/logos; use SF Symbols and original assets.
- Show FloodWait/429 with the seconds; never retry without a bound.

Hard limits (never build, never work around)
- Automatic saving of deleted messages; saving self-destructing media; bypassing protected content, bans, privacy settings or rate limits.
- Unlocking Premium/Stars/gifts/payments without a real payment through Telegram; fake subscription state or fake payment UI.
- Mass-sending or automation that violates Telegram's API terms.
- Allowed: manual, user-initiated local save of a chosen message.

Per-phase deliverables
- Update `README.md` and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` with a "Feature -> Status (implemented / partial / unsupported + reason)" table.
- A short list of expected build risks and what to watch in the Codemagic log. If the owner pastes build errors, fix ALL of them in one round.
- Stop at the end of the phase.

